my name is Lee Smolin and I'm a  theoretical physicist I work at Kramer  Institute for Theoretical Physics in  Ontario Canada and I work on the most  fundamental problems about nature what  is time  what is space what is the universe as a  whole where did the universe come from  what started at what was before the Big  Bang  in physics we study systems we study the  air in this room we study the elementary  particles created by a collision at the  Large Hadron Collider we study the solar  system or the galaxy is a system of  stars and these are all parts of the  universe as a whole and it's very  important that we treat them as parts of  a larger universe so that for example  where they are or how much energy they  have is to find relative to the rest of  the universe that's outside the system  so the very way we describe parts of the  universe as related to the rest of the  universe is characteristic of the method  by which we describe nature in physics  and when we raise our ambition and we  come to the question of the universe as  a whole or a question that is about the  whole story of the whole universe like  the big burner have you ever started or  what was before the Big Bang we're  dealing for the first time with the  question of how to describe and make a  theory of the universe as a whole and  this is a very different kind of theory  a very different kind of questions and  the questions we used to dealing with  and therefore it's important to stop and  meditate for a while on what it means to  make a theory of the universe or any  system as a whole  when we describe a star we do reference  it wouldn't describe it with respect to  things outside of it we describe its  position relative to the position of an  observer who's outside of the star when  we describe its motion we're describing  its motion relative to something outside  of it and so on  and that works that's the legitimate way  to do science except for when we come to  the case of the universe as a whole and  then we have to describe a system that  contains everything there is nothing  outside the universe and there therefore  is nothing to describe the universe with  reference to and this means we need a  very different kind of structure a range  of a kind of method a different kind of  theory when we come to study the  universe as a whole  because there's a tradition of thinking  deeply seriously and critically that  goes back centuries which is what you  learn when you study philosophy now if  you're not interested in a fundamental  question like the nature of time or what  is matter or the nature of the universe  as a whole but you just want to apply  known laws to known phenomena to  understand the better to understand the  behavior of a solid or the gas or a star  or something isolated like that you can  proceed without much knowledge of  philosophy you can proceed pragmatically  we like to say but if you want to ask  the really deep questions there are  these traditions of human beings  grappling with the same questions they  go back hundreds of years and not only  is it good to know them because it gives  you a storehouse of ideas it allows you  to understand your own thinking because  what you discover is that unless you're  extraordinarily special and unusual your  thinking is shaped by the philosophical  legacy that underlies our civilization
 

